Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["12. HRP\[Dash]CO Pocket-to-Solvent Kinetic Model", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"28bf172c-3ec6-6245-8e20-cac8bf0dca65"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "This section represents HRP\[Dash]CO recovery after photodissociation by \
resolving the unbound population into CO retained in or near the distal \
pocket and CO that has escaped to a solvent-accessible state. This state \
decomposition is consistent with reported internal and solvent rebinding \
behavior in HRP and with observation of a photolysis-induced intraprotein CO \
state.",
 StyleBox["4,5",
  FontVariations -> {"Superscript" -> True}],
 " Required inputs are the initial photolyzed fraction, dissolved-CO \
concentration, geminate recombination rate, pocket-escape rate, bimolecular \
association rate and fit starting values, the measured HRP\[Dash]CO \
transient, the fitted conformer populations, and the concentration, \
path-length, band-center, width, and absorptivity values required for \
spectral prediction. The pocket population decays with rate kgem + kescape, \
whereas the solvent population is populated by escape and depleted with the \
pseudo-first-order rate kon[CO]; their sum determines the unbound fraction \
and the time-dependent bound-band bleach. Defined population and \
difference-absorbance functions provide the pocket, solvent, total-unbound, \
and spectral predictions when separately invoked, while nonlinear fits \
compare the complete pocket-plus-solvent response with a solvent-only \
exponential through their fitted parameters and Akaike information criteria. \
This comparison tests whether the transient resolves an internal branch \
beyond the slower solvent recovery. The population-function implementation \
includes the nearly degenerate-rate limit, whereas the closed-form expression \
used in the fit retains a denominator proportional to kon[CO] \[Minus] (kgem \
+ kescape); the fitted expression is therefore valid only while those rate \
combinations remain sufficiently separated. Interpretation also remains \
conditional on effectively constant CO concentration, physically admissible \
positive rates, fit identifiability, and the possibility that pocket dynamics \
are faster than the instrumental response."
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{
  3.995988494893503*^9},ExpressionUUID->"91d3bbc8-16c6-fe49-a804-\
0f65adb275e8"],

